@page "/preferences"
@using Memoria.Web.Extensibility

@* This browser's own settings, which the server neither keeps nor is told about. A page of their
   own rather than a tab on Settings: Settings is an Administrator's page, because what it installs
   runs on the host, and a preference is nobody else's business — every operator has a browser, so
   every operator can reach this. Under the operator's name in the header, beside sign-out, which
   is the other thing that is theirs alone. *@

<PageTitle>Preferences</PageTitle>

<nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="">Home</a>
    <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
    <span aria-current="page">Preferences</span>
</nav>

<h1>Preferences</h1>

@* The settings in the card the data pages read their rows in: a preference is a name with a value
   at the end of it, which is the shape of a row of stored data, and drawing it a second way would
   make one page out of two. Divs rather than a table because there is nothing to head — two rows
   of two, and the line under each is a sentence rather than a third column. *@
<div class="preferences">
    @* First, because it is the one preference that changes every page rather than the tables on
       some of them.

       Three choices rather than a switch between two: nothing chosen is its own answer — the
       operating system's — and it is the one the page opens at, so a reader who has never picked
       has something to go back to. That third state is stored as the absence of the value rather
       than the word "system", which is why picking it clears the key instead of writing to it.

       Rendered at System and corrected to what was stored once the page is running, like the two
       below. Without scripting the select does nothing, so it is hidden until the script that
       drives it has run — and the page still follows the operating system, which is what System
       says anyway. *@
    <div class="preference" data-preference-row="theme" hidden>
        <div class="control">
            <label for="theme">Theme</label>
            <select id="theme" data-preference="theme">
                <option value="system" selected>System</option>
                <option value="light">Light</option>
                <option value="dark">Dark</option>
            </select>
        </div>
        <p class="hint">
            How this application is coloured, in this browser. System follows the light or dark
            setting the operating system asks for and changes with it.
        </p>
    </div>

    @* The same picker as the one under a table, carrying the same class, because it is the same
       choice: what is picked here is what that picker opens at, and what is picked there is what
       stands here afterwards. There is no form and nothing to submit — the select is the setting,
       and it is written down as it changes.

       Rendered at the default and corrected to what was stored once the page is running. The
       server has no way to read this browser's storage, so the alternative is a select that says
       nothing until it is opened. *@
    <div class="preference">
        <div class="control page-size">
            <label for="rows-per-page">Rows per page</label>
            <select id="rows-per-page" data-preference="rows-per-page">
                @foreach (var option in InstanceQuery.PageSizes)
                {
                    <option value="@option" selected="@(option == InstanceQuery.DefaultPageSize)">@option</option>
                }
            </select>
        </div>
        <p class="hint">
            How much of a table one page holds, wherever a table is read. The picker under a table
            sets the same thing and opens at whatever stands here.
        </p>
    </div>

    @* The same setting as the "Don't show this again" under the note itself, reachable without
       having to meet the note again to change one's mind — which is the whole difficulty with a
       dismissal: once hidden, the thing that offered to hide it is gone. Unchecked as rendered and
       corrected once the page is running, like the picker above.

       The box sits beside the label rather than inside it, so it stands where the picker above it
       does and the two rows are read the same way round. It is still the label's own control —
       for names it, so the words still toggle it. *@
    <div class="preference">
        <div class="control">
            <label for="hide-ordering-notice">Hide the note about approximate ordering</label>
            <input type="checkbox" id="hide-ordering-notice"
                   data-preference="hide-ordering-notice" />
        </div>
        <p class="hint">
            Some stores cannot order a page fully and fall back to ordering by date alone. The
            events are all there; rows written at the same moment can move between pages.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<p class="hint">Stored in this browser. The server keeps none of it and is never told.</p>
